WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.360
A departed director, unpaid cast and crew, and&nbsp;
an outdated premise. These are just a few of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:11.040
the problems that plagued what would sadly turn&nbsp;
out to be Kirstie Alley's last theatrical film.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:17.760
The actress, who died of cancer on December 5,&nbsp;
2002, was best known for playing bar manager&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:23.520
Rebecca Howe on "Cheers" from 1987&nbsp;
until 1993. Alley won two Emmy Awards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:28.260
for the role. She also starred alongside John&nbsp;
Travolta in three "Look Who's Talking" films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:33.780
and on "Veronica's Closet" from 1997 until&nbsp;
2000, earning another Emmy nomination.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:39.780
Though she'd later appear in some TV projects such&nbsp;
as "Scream Queens," her film acting career came to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:45.660
an undistinguished end. Her last theatrical&nbsp;
film, 2015's "Accidental Love," was plagued&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:50.100
by budget issues and abandoned by director&nbsp;
David O. Russell before its limited release.

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:55.440
According to Entertainment Weekly, before his&nbsp;
own departure, Russell clashed with James Caan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:59.940
over Caan's character's death scene, leading&nbsp;
to the veteran actor leaving the movie and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:04.740
part being recast. The film, originally&nbsp;
titled "Nailed," was actually never even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:09.120
technically finished, and several key&nbsp;
actors, not including Alley, left the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:13.140
production because the studio reportedly&nbsp;
left some of the cast and crew unpaid.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:16.620
When the movie finally made it to&nbsp;
theaters, it was not received well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:21.300
earning the approval of just 8% of&nbsp;
critics and 15% of audience members&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:25.080
on Rotten Tomatoes. Richard Roeper&nbsp;
of the Chicago Sun-Times wrote:

00:01:31.408 --> 00:01:35.580
He went on to call the movie itself&nbsp;
"an unmitigated disaster," adding:

00:01:45.117 --> 00:01:49.500
Here's the plot: A skating food server&nbsp;
named Alice, played by Jessica Biel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.960
is accidentally shot in the head with a nail gun.

00:01:52.288 --> 00:01:53.968
"That's your brain, you know?"

00:01:53.968 --> 00:01:56.400
"Yeah, that's my brain, so&nbsp;
please stop pushing on it."

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:01.320
The movie follows her bizarre experiences&nbsp;
seeking treatment and a normal life afterward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:06.360
Those experiences include meeting a man with&nbsp;
a prolapsed colon, played by Tracy Morgan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:11.280
and seeking care from a reverend with a&nbsp;
persistent erection, played by Kurt Fuller.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:15.660
The film's attempted underlying message about&nbsp;
the lack of affordable health care in the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:21.240
States was simply buried under crude jokes like&nbsp;
these. Critic James Mottram wrote of the film,

00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:35.880
Many critics also noted that "Accidental&nbsp;
Love" was seemingly past its sell-by date.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:40.260
Filming started in 2008, the year&nbsp;
Barack Obama was elected president.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:44.640
By the time the movie was released&nbsp;
in 2015, the Affordable Care Act,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:49.860
or "Obamacare" as it's popularly known, had&nbsp;
been in effect for five years. The movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:54.278
was a satire of a health care system that&nbsp;
had changed drastically during production.

00:02:54.278 --> 00:02:58.320
"Wait! She has no insurance. Shut it down."

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:01.970
"Thank you, Arlene! You're an angel."

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:03.900
Bilge Ebiri of Vulture wrote,

00:03:18.284 --> 00:03:22.680
Prairie Miller of WBAI radio in&nbsp;
New York noted that the film was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:26.400
co-written by former Vice President&nbsp;
Al Gore's daughter Kristin Gore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:29.880
based on her novel "Sammy's&nbsp;
Hill". She wrote of the film,

00:03:33.294 --> 00:03:38.820
The stellar cast wasn't enough to save the film,&nbsp;
and neither was the onslaught of crass jokes. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:44.100
long and sad story of "Accidental Love" should&nbsp;
serve as a cautionary tale for studio executives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:49.200
directors, and actors alike to choose their&nbsp;
projects carefully. Although the careers of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:53.640
Alley, Biel, Morgan, James Marsden,&nbsp;
Beverly D'Angelo, Jake Gyllenhaal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:57.660
and many more were not permanently tarnished&nbsp;
by their appearances in "Accidental Love,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:02.040
the film's infamous production lives&nbsp;
on as a warning of what not to do.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:07.200
Fortunately, Alley completed the Lifetime TV&nbsp;
movie "You Can't Take My Daughter" five years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:11.160
after the debacle that was "Accidental&nbsp;
Love." If not for the former film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:15.900
as well as appearances in TV shows like&nbsp;
"Flaked," the movie once known as "Nailed!"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:20.580
would have been the last memory we have of a&nbsp;
talented, versatile, and accomplished actor.

